Neurogenic Shock (Cervical Spine Trauma)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Distributive Shock
Profound hypotension, Paradoxical bradycardia, Warm/flushed skin below injury level

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-030
Reason for Visit: Immediate medical intervention requested by family for an elderly patient who suffered a high-impact fall down stairs, resulting in an inability to move limbs and severe presyncope, requiring stabilization prior to ambulance arrival.
Location: Private residence (bottom of internal staircase)
Present at Visit: Patient and distressed spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a catastrophic fall mechanism followed immediately by complete paralysis and fading consciousness. This clinical picture mandated an immediate, hyper-urgent on-site medical response (occurring concurrently with Priority 1 ambulance dispatch) to protect the cervical spine, assess for life-threatening distributive shock, and secure the airway prior to standard emergency service arrival.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Inability to move arms or legs, severe dizziness, and neck pain after a fall. Patient states in a faint voice, I tripped down the top stairs, my neck snapped backward, and now I can't feel my body and the room is going dark.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms occurred immediately following a blunt trauma mechanical fall down a flight of 12 wooden stairs 15 minutes ago. The patient reports a sudden, complete loss of motor function and sensation below the neck. The spouse notes the patient has become increasingly lethargic and complains of profound dizziness, unable to mount a tachycardic response to pain or the presumed hypovolemia. There is a complete lack of feeling in the chest, abdomen, and all four extremities.
Functional Status: Immobile on the floor, exhibiting flaccid tetraplegia; completely dependent for airway protection and stabilization.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate, critical life-threat due to high cervical spinal cord injury and resulting hemodynamic collapse; spouse is panicked but following commands.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 75/40 mmHg | HR: 52 bpm | Temp: 36.0 C | RR: 12 bpm | SpO2: 93% | Wt: 80.0 Kg
General: Patient is lying supine at the base of the stairs, conscious but obtunded, exhibiting shallow, diaphragmatic breathing.
Physical Exam: Neurological: Flaccid paralysis and absent reflexes are observed below the clavicles. A loss of motor function and sensation below the suspected level of trauma is acutely present. Cardiovascular: The diagnostic triad is clinically unmissable: there is profound hypotension unresponsive to initial passive leg raising. Paradoxical bradycardia (HR 52 bpm) is present despite severe shock, due to the total loss of sympathetic cardiac innervation from the high cervical transection. Integumentary: Examination reveals warm, dry, and flushed skin, particularly below the level of the spinal cord injury, due to massive loss of vasomotor tone. There is an absence of compensatory diaphoresis in the affected dermatomes, and early impaired thermoregulation resulting in poikilothermia is suspected.
Environmental Observations: Patient is stabilized on the floor. Immediate area cleared of debris to allow unhindered paramedic access.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes an antihypertensive (Ramipril), which exacerbates the current profound vasodilation, but no beta-blockers.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Neurogenic Shock (Cervical Spine Trauma) - Status: Acute/Life-threatening
Differentials:

Hypovolemic/Hemorrhagic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of paradoxical bradycardia and warm, flushed peripheries, whereas hemorrhagic shock classically presents with marked tachycardia and cold, clammy, pale skin.

Cardiogenic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear traumatic mechanism of injury, profound peripheral vasodilation (warm skin), and complete flaccid tetraplegia isolating the spinal cord as the primary lesion.

Simple Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, persistent hypotension combined with hard neurological deficits (complete motor and sensory loss), defining a catastrophic structural injury.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient has suffered a catastrophic cervical spine injury resulting in immediate, life-threatening Neurogenic Shock. The abrupt loss of descending sympathetic vascular tone has caused massive peripheral vasodilation and distributive fluid pooling, resulting in profound hypotension. Concurrently, the loss of sympathetic cardiac innervation has caused unopposed vagal tone, producing the paradoxical bradycardia. This hemodynamic collapse severely compromises central perfusion. Immediate on-site medical presence was critical to execute strict manual in-line stabilization of the cervical spine, maintain airway patency, and provide advanced clinical handover to the arriving intensive care paramedics. Delaying or waiting solely for standard emergency transport without immediate on-site intervention risked fatal secondary spinal cord ischemia or asphyxiation. The expected investigation results include acute severe spinal cord compression or transection at or above the T6 level on emergent magnetic resonance imaging that would assist in confirming the diagnosis at the trauma center.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated and maintained strict manual in-line stabilization (MILS) of the cervical spine. Maintained patient strictly flat to maximize cerebral perfusion. Applied high-flow oxygen. Coordinated directly with the incoming Priority 1 ambulance crew for urgent vasopressor support (e.g., Noradrenaline) and spinal immobilization equipment.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Directed to the spouse assisting): To protect the spinal cord from permanent severing, you must place your hands exactly where I instruct and hold the head completely rigid. Do not look away or release pressure. When you hold the head perfectly still, consciously recognize that your physical strength is the only thing preserving the remaining nerve function, providing the intense focus needed to maintain this vital immobilization until the stretcher arrives.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Trauma Paramedics and subsequent Major Trauma Center teams.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Paramedic response is currently arriving on scene. Clinical management is transitioning to advanced trauma life support protocols.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a catastrophic traumatic emergency requiring immediate transport to a tertiary spinal trauma center.
Clinical Course: The spinal cord injury has severed the brain's connection to the blood vessels and heart. The blood vessels have lost their tension and expanded massively, dropping the blood pressure critically low. Immediate intensive care using specialized intravenous medications to squeeze the blood vessels back tight is required to preserve brain and organ function.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not move any part of your body. Do not attempt to lift your head or turn to look at anyone. Absolute stillness is critical to prevent further spinal cord damage.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fall has injured the nerves in your neck, causing a massive drop in blood pressure and paralysis. We are holding your neck strictly immobile to prevent further damage while the ambulance crew prepares medications to artificially raise your blood pressure.
Actionable Steps: Remain completely still. Focus on taking steady, shallow breaths. Let the medical teams handle all movement.

